WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.202
Two maintenance tickets refer to the same building,
and that building record refers to its active

00:00:06.202 --> 00:00:06.714
tickets.

00:00:06.714 --> 00:00:11.254
Naively nesting the entire building inside each
ticket duplicates data.

00:00:11.254 --> 00:00:16.881
Following the building’s ticket references
recursively can also create an endless cycle.

00:00:16.881 --> 00:00:22.124
The in-memory structure is a graph, while ordinary
nested JSON values form a tree.

00:00:22.124 --> 00:00:28.831
Serialization must decide how to represent identity
and relationships instead of blindly walking

00:00:28.831 --> 00:00:29.809
every pointer.

00:00:29.809 --> 00:00:36.726
One representation emits building B9 once, emits
tickets T1 and T2 once, and links each ticket with

00:00:36.726 --> 00:00:37.704
buildingId B9.

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:44.062
The client resolves those identifiers using the
agreed response structure or additional API

00:00:44.062 --> 00:00:44.691
requests.

00:00:44.724 --> 00:00:50.990
This reduces duplication but requires a rule for
missing references and for when related objects

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:52.100
become available.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:58.366
Also distinguish text length from byte length: a
title containing accented characters can occupy

00:00:58.366 --> 00:01:01.108
more UTF-8 bytes than its character count.

00:01:01.108 --> 00:01:06.722
Schema choices, reference rules and encoding all
belong to the data exchange contract.

00:01:06.724 --> 00:01:12.146
The maintenance team supports a browser dashboard
and an internal batch client.

00:01:12.146 --> 00:01:18.873
It first lists requirements: available client
libraries, message sizes, streaming needs, debugging

00:01:18.873 --> 00:01:21.139
tools and expected compatibility.

00:01:21.139 --> 00:01:27.316
A familiar HTTP and JSON interface may make
integration easy, while a schema-driven binary

00:01:27.316 --> 00:01:30.748
representation may be useful for another workload.

00:01:30.748 --> 00:01:37.722
The decision should compare complete systems under
realistic requests, not assume that a smaller

00:01:37.722 --> 00:01:41.717
encoded message automatically makes the service
faster.

00:01:41.717 --> 00:01:43.969
Keep the design layers visible.

00:01:43.969 --> 00:01:50.870
REST describes architectural constraints, HTTP
defines protocol semantics, and JSON or Protocol

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:52.323
Buffers encode data.

00:01:52.323 --> 00:01:59.151
An RPC framework supplies another set of conventions
and tools; binary serialization can still

00:01:59.151 --> 00:02:02.638
travel over HTTP, as in common gRPC deployments.

00:02:02.648 --> 00:02:07.947
Reusing HTTP infrastructure also means respecting
its caching and retry rules.

00:02:07.947 --> 00:02:14.604
Before changing formats, measure where time is
spent: if most latency is a database wait, saving a

00:02:14.604 --> 00:02:16.846
few bytes may have little effect.

00:02:16.846 --> 00:02:22.892
Select an interface that meets the operational and
compatibility needs you actually have.
